50 && head -5 events.csv 2>/dev/null"], ["Bash", "cat events.csv && cat README.md && grep -rl events.csv . --exclude-dir=.git | head"]], "final_message": "The cause is almost certainly mixed timestamp formats in `events.csv`. Its `ts` column has three formats:\n\n| id | `ts` | Format | Actual UTC time |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `2026-03-01T10:00:00Z` | UTC (`Z`) | 10:00 |\n| 2 | `2026-03-01T10:05:00` | **No offset** (naive) | Unknown. 10:05 only if the source was UTC. |\n| 3 | `2026-03-01T04:10:00-06:00` | Offset of \u221206:00 | 10:10 |\n\nRows 1 and 3 are consistent: 10:00Z and 10:10Z. Row 2 has no offset, so the tool that reads it has to guess:\n\n- **Parsed as local time**: pandas, JS `Date` and most BI tools do this for naive strings. On a machine in UTC\u22126 it becomes 16:05Z. On UTC+2 it becomes 08:05Z. The point lands hours away from its neighbours.\n- **Dropped offsets**: some tools read `04:10:00-06:00` as 04:10 and discard the offset. Row 3 then shows up 6 hours early.\n- **Different viewers**: because naive values follow the viewer's timezone, two people can see different charts from the same file.\n\nThe size of the jump should match the UTC offset of the machine or tool that made the chart. Row 3's six-hour shift is the clearest case.\n\n**Fix:**\n1. Find out what timezone the source system used for naive timestamps. Row 2 is probably UTC, but the file doesn't say.\n2. Normalize on load, for example `pd.to_datetime(df.ts, utc=True)` in pandas. This treats naive values as UTC and converts offset values correctly. If the naive rows aren't UTC, localize them first with `tz_localize(...)`.\n3. Better still, fix the export so every row carries an explicit offset or `Z`.\n\nThis file has only three rows, so I can't tell whether the naive timestamps are the main problem in your real data. I also haven't seen the chart code. If you point me to the script or the export job, I can check how it parses `ts` and patch it.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "befo